 my daily driver for a few weeks now.<p>I think I improved on the concept in a few ways, focusing on the ergonomics I always wished I had:<p>I show you what the agents are up to, along with a running feed of their activity.\nThere's also a simple shortcut (F1) to go to the next unread agent.<p>I use multiple accounts, so I've added unified launcher for Codex/Claude Code/Pi.\nTheres some careful ergonomics there: fast search and resume over sessions, inline usage tracking etc.<p>Many small quality of life improvements to the terminal experience:\n- Links can be copied with a click, so no need to drag-select over many lines\n- cmd+v just pastes images (even on SSH)\n- images have a thumbnail preview.\n- Built-in CPU/Memory/GPU usage tracking, broken down by workspace<p>I assume you have access to cheap inference (Luna/Haiku/etc).\nIt just reuses the agents to organize things for you: what is this http link? what is this worktree about? what is each agent doing? \nIt's small but it made my environment feel more cozy.<p>On a personal note, I've been on a frustrating sick leave for a while now.\nI created Corgi to challenge myself, and see if I can go back.\nThe answer is more mixed than I had hoped, but I am happy with the result and thankful for the experience.<p>I hope you like it! a few friends &amp; myself have been using it for a few weeks and it's been pretty nice.\nThere's still a lot to improve and some rough edges, so feedback would be greatly appreciated.<p>Thank you :)"},"title":{"matchLevel":"none","matchedWords":[],"value":"Show HN: Corgi, a helpful {agent, terminal} multiplexer"},"url":{"matchLevel":"none","matchedWords":[],"value":"https://corgi.computer/"}},"_tags":["story","author_eiopa","story_50214373","show_hn","front_page"],"author":"eiopa","children":[50217001,50217236,50217652,50217777,50217919,50218291,50218331,50218790],"created_at":"2026-10-23T19:46:38Z","created_at_i":1792784798,"num_comments":18,"objectID":"50214373","points":73,"story_id":50214373,"story_text":"Hey everyone,<p>Inspired by Herdr, I&#x27;ve built an agent multiplexer that I&#x27;ve been using as my daily driver for a few weeks now.<p>I think I improved on the concept in a few ways, focusing on the ergonomics I always wished I had:<p>I show you what the agents are up to, along with a running feed of their activity.\nThere&#x27;s also a simple shortcut (F1) to go to the next unread agent.<p>I use multiple accounts, so I&#x27;ve added unified launcher for Codex&#x2F;Claude Code&#x2F;Pi.\nTheres some careful ergonomics there: fast search and resume over sessions, inline usage tracking etc.<p>Many small quality of life improvements to the terminal experience:\n- Links can be copied with a click, so no need to drag-select over many lines\n- cmd+v just pastes images (even on SSH)\n- images have a thumbnail preview.\n- Built-in CPU&#x2F;Memory&#x2F;GPU usage tracking, broken down by workspace<p>I assume you have access to cheap inference (Luna&#x2F;Haiku&#x2F;etc).\nIt just reuses the agents to organize things for you: what is this http link? what is this worktree about? what is each agent doing? \nIt&#x27;s small but it made my environment feel more cozy.<p>On a personal note, I&#x27;ve been on a frustrating sick leave for a while now.\nI created Corgi to challenge myself, and see if I can go back.\nThe answer is more mixed than I had hoped, but I am happy with the result and thankful for the experience.<p>I hope you like it! a few friends &amp; myself have been using it for a few weeks and it&#x27;s been pretty nice.\nThere&#x27;s still a lot to improve and some rough edges, so feedback would be greatly appreciated.<p>Thank you :)","title":"Show HN: Corgi, a helpful {agent, terminal} multiplexer","updated_at":"2026-10-24T07:16:57Z","url":"https://corgi.computer/"},{"_highlightResult":{"author":{"matchLevel":"none","matchedWords":[],"value":"stmonty"},"title":{"matchLevel":"none","matchedWords":[],"value":"How close can pure functional programm